  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nRidging dominates the western two-thirds of the CONUS as a \nstrengthening trough digs into the Great Lakes region. A 990mb low \npressure is centered north of the Lakes with a cold frontal boundary \nextending across the Ohio Valley and into Oklahoma. The frontal \nboundary passed over the forecast area this morning, turning winds \nout of the northeast. Gusts this afternoon have reached between 15-\n25 mph behind the front. The drier air has been slower to filter in, \ndewpoints across central Kansas have fallen into the 50s with \nportions of south-central Kansas now in the 60s. Far southern and \nsoutheast Kansas dewpoints remain in the 70s, which when combined \nwith temperatures near 100 degrees is leading heat indices over 110 \ndegrees. Temperatures generally north of Hwy 50 will only reach into \nthe middle 90s this afternoon, with upper 90s and lower 100s further \nsouth. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for southern and \nsoutheast Kansas from 12-8pm and a Heat Advisory is in place for \nportions of south-central and southeast Kansas. \n\nFor Wednesday and Thursday, the forecast area will remain on the \nnortheast edge of the ridge with the ridge axis extending from the \nSouthern Plains northwestward into the Northern Rockies. \nTemperatures for mid week will top out near normal with highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s. Drier air behind the front will also make \nthings feel more comfortable as dewpoints in the upper 50s to lower \n60s are anticipated. A shortwave trough will begin to dig into the \nHigh Plains on Wednesday, traversing the Central Plains on Thursday \ninto Thursday night. This feature, along with a strengthening LLJ \neach night, will support rain and storm chances Wed. and Thu. nights \nfrom northern into eastern Kansas. Then a few shower and storm \nchances may linger in eastern Kansas on Friday afternoon and \nevening before scooting to the east. Severe weather is not \nanticipated with any of this activity.\n\nThe ridge will begin to build back over the region on Friday, with \nmid-level heights increasing through Saturday. The ridge axis looks \nto be situated just to our west for Sunday into early next week. \nTemperatures for Friday through the end of the forecast period look \nto gradually increase each day, with 100s possible again by the end \nof next weekend and into Monday. With the ridge nearly overhead for \nthe end of the forecast time frame, negligible rain chances and \nmostly clear skies are expected to return.",
